\title{A Non-Abelianity Certificate for Jones Braid Operators}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-19}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Topological quantum computation proposes to encode and process quantum
information in the braiding of non-abelian anyons, with the Jones representation
of the braid group --- built from the Temperley--Lieb algebra --- providing the
unitary gates. The computational power of such a scheme depends, at its very
foundation, on the \emph{non-abelian} character of the braid image: if the map
from braid generators to gates collapsed non-commuting data into commuting
operators, the model would be powerless. We isolate and prove the algebraic
mechanism that guarantees this collapse does \emph{not} happen. For a field $K$,
an associative $K$-algebra $A$, a unit $u \in \Kx$, and an element $X \in A$,
define the \textbf{Jones operator} $\jop(u, X) = u\cdot\one + u^{-1}\cdot X$,
modeling the image of a braid generator built from the Temperley--Lieb generator
$X$. We establish an exact commutator identity
$\comm{\jop(u,X)}{\jop(u,Y)} = u^{-2}\cdot\comm{X}{Y}$
and deduce the non-abelianity equivalence: the Jones operators of $X$ and $Y$
commute if and only if $X$ and $Y$ commute. The proof rests on the injectivity of
scalar multiplication by the nonzero scalar $u^{-2}$. We connect this
unit-parametrized operator to a field-element-parametrized operator from an
existing Temperley--Lieb/braid formalization, transport the equivalence across
that bridge, and exhibit an explicit $2\times2$ rational example certifying
non-commutativity for every rational unit $u$ simultaneously. We make no claim of
density, universality, or topology beyond this algebraic certificate, and we
delineate the additional structure required to ascend toward genuine universality
theorems. All results have been formally verified.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation: braiding as computation}
In two spatial dimensions the worldlines of indistinguishable particles can be
knotted, and the exchange statistics of such particles are governed not by the
symmetric group but by the \emph{braid group} $B_n$. For \textbf{non-abelian
anyons}, exchanging two particles acts on a degenerate ground-state space by a
\emph{unitary matrix} rather than a mere phase, and distinct braids generally act
by distinct, non-commuting unitaries. This is the basis of \textbf{topological
quantum computation} (TQC): information is stored in the fusion space of several
anyons, and gates are implemented by braiding them. Because the unitary depends
only on the homotopy class of the braid, the computation is intrinsically
protected against local perturbations.

The relevant unitary representations are the \textbf{Jones representations} of the
braid group, constructed via the \textbf{Temperley--Lieb (TL) algebra}. A braid
generator $\sigma_i$ is sent to an operator of the affine form $A\cdot\one +
A^{-1}\cdot e_i$, where $e_i$ is a TL generator; at suitable roots of unity these
operators are unitary, and the \textbf{Fibonacci} model (parameter $k=5$) is
conjectured to be universal for quantum computation on $B_4$.

\subsection{The question this paper answers}
Universality is a deep analytic statement (density of a generated subgroup in a
unitary group). Beneath any density theorem lies a purely algebraic prerequisite:
the representation must be \textbf{non-abelian}, i.e.\ it must send non-commuting
generators to non-commuting operators. If this failed, the image would be
commutative, no density would be possible, and TQC would be impossible in this
model. We answer the prerequisite question completely:
\begin{quote}
Does the Jones map $X \mapsto \jop(u,X)$ preserve, and reflect, the commutativity
of generators?
\end{quote}
The answer is yes, in the strongest possible (biconditional) sense.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item An exact commutator identity (Theorem~\ref{thm:comm}): the Jones map sends
    $\comm{X}{Y}$ to $u^{-2}\cdot\comm{X}{Y}$, with no error terms.
  \item A non-abelianity equivalence (Theorem~\ref{thm:equiv}): $\jop(u,X)$ and
    $\jop(u,Y)$ commute iff $X$ and $Y$ commute.
  \item A scalar-cancellation lemma (Lemma~\ref{lem:cancel}).
  \item A bridge (Theorem~\ref{thm:bridge}) to an existing TL/braid formalization
    and a transported equivalence (Theorem~\ref{thm:transport}).
  \item A concrete rational certificate (Theorems~\ref{thm:gen}--\ref{thm:jnc}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Preliminaries and definitions}
Throughout, $K$ is a field, $A$ is an associative unital $K$-algebra (possibly
noncommutative), and $\Kx$ denotes the units of $K$. For $u\in\Kx$ we write
$u\in K$ for its value and $u^{-1}$ for the value of its inverse; thus
$u\,u^{-1}=1$. We write $\cdot$ for the scalar action and juxtaposition for
algebra multiplication. The \textbf{commutator} is $\comm{P}{Q}:=PQ-QP$.

\begin{definition}[Jones operator]\label{def:jop}
For $u\in\Kx$ and $X\in A$,
\[ \jop(u,X) := u\cdot\one + u^{-1}\cdot X \in A. \]
\end{definition}

This is the algebraic image of a braid generator in the Jones representation, with
the parameter promoted to a unit so the weight is invertible by construction.

\begin{definition}[Catalog Jones operator]\label{def:jcat}
For $A_0\in K$ and $X\in R$,
\[ \jcat(A_0,X) := A_0\cdot\one + A_0^{-1}\cdot X. \]
\end{definition}

When $A_0=u$ for a unit $u$, $A_0^{-1}=u^{-1}$, so the definitions agree
(Theorem~\ref{thm:bridge}). The companion formalization establishes, in this same
language, the adjacent braid relation
$\jcat(A_0,X)\jcat(A_0,Y)\jcat(A_0,X)=\jcat(A_0,Y)\jcat(A_0,X)\jcat(A_0,Y)$
under the TL relations $X^2=\delta\cdot X$, $XYX=X$, $YXY=Y$ with loop value
$\delta=-(A_0^2+A_0^{-2})$, distant commutation for commuting generators, and the
two-sided inverse $\jcat(A_0,X)\cdot\mathrm{jonesInv}(A_0,X)=1$ with
$\mathrm{jonesInv}(A_0,X)=A_0^{-1}\cdot\one+A_0\cdot X$.

\section{The non-abelianity certificate}

\begin{lemma}[Unit scalar multiplication is injective]\label{lem:cancel}
For any unit $v\in\Kx$ and any $z\in A$, $\;v\cdot z = 0 \iff z = 0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The reverse direction is immediate. For the forward direction, suppose
$v\cdot z=0$ and apply $v^{-1}$: $v^{-1}\cdot(v\cdot z)=(v^{-1}v)\cdot z=1\cdot
z=z$, while $v^{-1}\cdot 0=0$. Hence $z=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact commutator identity]\label{thm:comm}
For any unit $u\in\Kx$ and any $X,Y\in A$,
\[ \jop(u,X)\,\jop(u,Y) - \jop(u,Y)\,\jop(u,X) = \bigl(u^{-1}u^{-1}\bigr)\cdot(XY-YX). \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $a:=u$, $b:=u^{-1}$. By bilinearity,
\[ (a\cdot\one+b\cdot X)(a\cdot\one+b\cdot Y)=a^2\cdot\one+ab\cdot Y+ab\cdot X+b^2\cdot XY, \]
\[ (a\cdot\one+b\cdot Y)(a\cdot\one+b\cdot X)=a^2\cdot\one+ab\cdot X+ab\cdot Y+b^2\cdot YX. \]
Subtracting, the $a^2\cdot\one$ terms and the symmetric cross terms
$ab\cdot X+ab\cdot Y$ cancel, leaving $b^2\cdot(XY-YX)=u^{-2}\cdot(XY-YX)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Non-abelianity equivalence]\label{thm:equiv}
For any unit $u\in\Kx$ and any $X,Y\in A$,
\[ \jop(u,X)\,\jop(u,Y)=\jop(u,Y)\,\jop(u,X) \iff XY=YX. \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Commutativity is equivalent to the vanishing of the commutator, which by
Theorem~\ref{thm:comm} equals $u^{-2}\cdot(XY-YX)$. Since $u^{-2}$ is the value of
a unit, Lemma~\ref{lem:cancel} gives that this vanishes iff $XY-YX=0$, i.e.\ iff
$XY=YX$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The forward direction rules out hidden abelianization (the map cannot manufacture
spurious commutativity); the reverse direction recovers distant commutation. The
unit hypothesis is exactly the regime relevant physically, where the parameter is
a root of unity --- always a unit.
\end{remark}

\section{Bridge to the Temperley--Lieb/braid formalization}

\begin{theorem}[Specialization bridge]\label{thm:bridge}
For any unit $u\in\Kx$ and any $X\in A$, $\;\jop(u,X)=\jcat(u,X)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Unfold both definitions: the left side is $u\cdot\one+u^{-1}\cdot X$, the right
side $u\cdot\one+(u)^{-1}\cdot X$, and $u^{-1}=(u)^{-1}$ for a unit.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Transported equivalence]\label{thm:transport}
For any unit $u\in\Kx$ and any $X,Y\in A$,
\[ \jcat(u,X)\,\jcat(u,Y)=\jcat(u,Y)\,\jcat(u,X) \iff XY=YX. \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Rewrite each catalog operator via Theorem~\ref{thm:bridge} and apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:equiv}.
\end{proof}

\section{A concrete rational certificate}
Instantiate $K=\mathbb{Q}$ and $A=M_2(\mathbb{Q})$.

\begin{definition}[Explicit generators]
\[ X=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\0&0\end{pmatrix}, \qquad Y=\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\1&0\end{pmatrix}. \]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:gen}
$XY\neq YX$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$XY=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&0\\0&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)$ and
$YX=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&0\\0&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)$ differ in their
$(0,0)$ entry.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:jnc}
For every rational unit $u\in\mathbb{Q}^{\times}$,
\[ \jop(u,X)\,\jop(u,Y)\neq\jop(u,Y)\,\jop(u,X). \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By the contrapositive of Theorem~\ref{thm:equiv} the operators commute iff
$XY=YX$, which fails by Theorem~\ref{thm:gen}; the conclusion holds uniformly in
$u$ since the equivalence is parameter-independent.
\end{proof}

\section{A fully worked numerical example at the Fibonacci weight}
For $u=e^{2\pi i/5}$ ($k=5$) acting on the nilpotent generators of
Definition~5.1, write $a=e^{2\pi i/5}$, $b=e^{-2\pi i/5}$, so $u^{-2}=b^2=
e^{-4\pi i/5}$ has modulus $1$. Then
\[
\jop(u,X)=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\0&a\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\jop(u,Y)=\begin{pmatrix}a&0\\b&a\end{pmatrix},
\]
\[
\jop(u,X)\jop(u,Y)=\begin{pmatrix}a^2+b^2&ab\\ab&a^2\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\jop(u,Y)\jop(u,X)=\begin{pmatrix}a^2&ab\\ab&a^2+b^2\end{pmatrix},
\]
so
\[
\comm{\jop(u,X)}{\jop(u,Y)}=b^2\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix}
=u^{-2}\,(XY-YX),
\]
since $XY-YX=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{smallmatrix}\right)$. As
$b^2\neq0$ the commutator is nonzero (the gates do not commute), and any other
weight changes only the scalar $b^2$, never whether the commutator vanishes ---
the parameter-independence of Theorem~\ref{thm:equiv} visible in one formula.

\section{Algorithms}
\subsection{Commutator-identity verifier}
Given $u,X,Y$, compute $J_X=u I+u^{-1}X$, $J_Y=u I+u^{-1}Y$, and compare
$J_X J_Y-J_Y J_X$ with $u^{-2}(XY-YX)$ within tolerance. Cost $O(d^\omega)$ for
$d\times d$ matrices.

\subsection{Non-abelianity decision procedure}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:equiv} the weight $u$ is irrelevant, so deciding whether two
Jones operators commute reduces to testing $XY=YX$ on the generators: cost
$O(d^\omega)$, independent of $u$ and requiring no operator assembly.

\section{Applications and significance}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Foundational soundness of TQC gate sets.} The certificate
    guarantees that any unit-weight Jones representation inherits exactly the
    non-commutativity of its TL generators.
  \item \textbf{A $u$-independent commutativity oracle.} Commutativity of Jones
    gates is decidable from the generators, independently of the parameter.
  \item \textbf{Separation of universal and representation-dependent content.} The
    exact identity factors the action on commutators into a universal piece
    $\comm{X}{Y}$ and a scalar weight $u^{-2}$.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Discussion and limitations}
The results establish a non-abelianity certificate and nothing more: no density,
no universality, no topology. Density requires control of spectra and a unitary
structure, neither used here. What the certificate provides is the rigorous base
camp, ruling out hidden abelianization and showing that non-commutativity at the
level of TL generators is transmitted intact to the operators a device would
execute.

\section{Future directions}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{From a certificate to a non-abelian group image.} Lift the
    equivalence to the subgroup of $A^{\times}$ generated by Jones images of a
    family of TL generators, using pairwise control of commutativity.
  \item \textbf{The full Temperley--Lieb algebra with loop parameter $\delta$.}
    Connect $X^2=\delta X$, $XYX=X$, $\delta=-(A_0^2+A_0^{-2})$ to the
    parameter-free commutator identity, deriving the braid relation from a
    presented $TL_n(\delta)$.
  \item \textbf{Spectral and unitarity refinements toward density.} Make $A$ a
    finite-dimensional $C^*$-algebra and compute the eigenvalues of $\jop(u,X)$ as
    the affine image $u+u^{-1}\lambda$ of those of $X$.
  \item \textbf{Explicit Fibonacci-parameter representations over cyclotomic
    fields.} Specialize $u$ to a primitive root of unity (e.g.\ $k=5$).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
The exact commutator identity $\comm{\jop(u,X)}{\jop(u,Y)}=u^{-2}\cdot\comm{X}{Y}$
and the equivalence $\jop(u,X)\leftrightarrow\jop(u,Y)$ commute iff $X\leftrightarrow
Y$ commute show that the Jones map is a faithful, two-way translator of
commutativity, with the unit weight contributing only an invertible scalar. While
density and universality lie beyond this certificate, it is exactly the foundation
on which those results must be built.

